    "description": "Iran experienced a major transition from large families to much lower fertility during the late twentieth century. Recent international estimates show a temporary increase during the mid-2010s followed by renewed decline, making the definition and timing of fertility measures essential to understanding the trend.",
    "tags": ["Fertility", "Demographic transition", "Family formation", "Population estimates", "Reproductive behavior"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T03:57:54Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T03:57:54Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Fertility in Iran: Family Formation and Demographic Change",
      "description": "Understand Iran’s fertility transition, recent annual estimates, and why births per woman differ from birth counts and population growth."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "understanding-fertility",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the fertility rate measures",
        "content": "Iran’s fertility history concerns both changing family formation and a statistical measure that needs careful interpretation. The total fertility rate estimates how many children a hypothetical woman would have if she experienced a particular year’s age-specific fertility rates throughout her childbearing life. It summarizes the conditions of one period; it does not follow an actual generation of women until their families are complete. [s2]\n\nThat distinction matters when births are postponed or concentrated at particular ages. A period rate describes the current pattern of childbearing, rather than establishing the final number of children that today’s young adults will have. It also differs from a birth count: the number of births depends on how many women are at childbearing ages as well as their fertility rates. Stanford’s historical analysis of Iran explicitly identifies this cohort effect when explaining changes in registered births. [s2][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "historical-transition",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A transition involving more than one cause",
        "content": "Research by Mohammad Jalal Abbasi-Shavazi, Meimanat Hosseini-Chavoshi, and Peter McDonald describes Iran’s movement to below-replacement fertility around the turn of the century. Their published abstract identifies later marriage, smaller desired families, and reduced progression to subsequent births as important pathways. It also connects women’s increasing education with changing expectations about employment and family life. These findings concern a historical transition, rather than proving that any single factor explains every subsequent annual movement. [s3]\n\nThe transition also reached rural communities. Stanford’s retrospective analysis emphasizes that the fertility decline extended beyond metropolitan Iran. A national rate nevertheless remains an average: it cannot establish that every province, settlement, or social group shares the same reproductive pattern. Regional comparisons require estimates from matching reference years and methods. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "recent-estimates",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A temporary rise followed by renewed decline",
        "content": "The inspected World Development Indicators series, distributed through the Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis’s FRED database, estimates 1.851 births per woman in 2012. It rises to 2.054 in 2016, then falls to 1.684 in 2024. The series therefore shows neither an uninterrupted recent decline nor a sustained return to higher fertility. [s1]\n\nThese are source-produced demographic estimates, not a new calculation from birth registrations undertaken for this page. Their displayed precision should not be mistaken for equivalent certainty about individual reproductive decisions. The chart preserves the published values while leaving later unavailable years empty. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "fertility-trend",
        "type": "chart",
        "content": {
          "type": "line",
          "title": "Iran: estimated total fertility rate, 2012–2026",
          "dataset_id": "iran-total-fertility",
          "x_field": "period_label",
          "series_ids": ["total-fertility-rate"],
          "x_axis_label": "Gregorian reporting year",
          "y_axis_label": "Births per woman",
          "y_axis_min": 0,
          "show_legend": false,
          "connect_nulls": false,
          "interpretation": "The published estimates rise from 2012 to 2016 and then decline through 2024. No values are supplied for 2025 or 2026."
        },
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-consequences",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading fertility alongside age structure",
        "content": "Fertility contributes to the size of future generations, but a fertility rate alone cannot establish when a population will stop growing. The ages of existing residents matter: a large generation reaching adulthood can sustain substantial birth numbers even with smaller families. Stanford’s analysis treats this population momentum separately from fertility itself. [s4]\n\nFor readers, the useful conclusion is to distinguish three questions: how intensely childbearing occurs in a particular year, how many births occur, and how the population’s age composition changes. They are connected, but they require different evidence. This page documents the first question; the linked age-structure and aging pages address the broader demographic setting. [s2][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s4"]
      }
    ],
